    "output": "Yes, research documents this pattern. Studies show that stereotypes linking Black individuals—particularly men—to criminality and violence persist in urban contexts and demonstrably affect social expectations, policing practices, hiring decisions, and educational discipline. These stereotypes influence everything from how people perceive ambiguous behavior to disparities in criminal justice outcomes, even when controlling for actual crime rates. Importantly, scholars distinguish between these *perceptions and stereotypes* versus actual crime data, which shows that structural factors (poverty, disinvestment, concentrated disadvantage) better explain crime patterns than race itself.",
